WEBVTT

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:02.530
You are in a race with the hackers all the
time.

00:00:02.530 --> 00:00:04.190
It can take over the entire server.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:08.080
That's the moment when they just put on
this black screen and say, we want to get

00:00:08.080 --> 00:00:11.510
free Bitcoins from you for giving you back
access.

00:00:11.510 --> 00:00:14.550
And we did that for 400 websites within 24
hours.

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:15.710
Wow.

00:00:15.710 --> 00:00:17.790
All thanks to Grok and Claude AI.

00:00:17.790 --> 00:00:19.230
Jesus.

00:00:19.230 --> 00:00:22.030
You were on that? Just by yourself?

00:00:22.030 --> 00:00:25.510
A colleague and I did it, the two of us.

00:00:25.510 --> 00:00:29.840
How many people would it have required to
do that pre-

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:35.490
AI? I don't know. But even if you know
what the

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:38.830
issue is, you still need to know how to
solve it.

00:00:38.830 --> 00:00:43.150
If I just download the script and have
Grok locally scrape the script and

00:00:43.150 --> 00:00:44.150
say, what is the issue?

00:00:44.150 --> 00:00:48.780
And I can even just download an entire
backup of all the websites and say, go

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:51.670
through the folders one by one and give me
a list.

00:00:51.670 --> 00:00:54.870
Within an hour, I know for 50 websites
what I need to update.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:56.030
That's nuts.

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.270
That would have been, like, days of work
before AI.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:03.230
Because the bug itself was already known
for more than a year.

00:01:03.230 --> 00:01:08.210
Yeah, exactly. And the same thing for all
of the operating system stuff that got

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:10.430
used in the last hack.

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:15.670
They were known issues for the last 10
years, but never fixed because it was

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:20.310
too complicated to exploit.

00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.710
Yes. Not anymore when you have Fable.

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.710
They make another suggestion, but they
look at the issue.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:30.070
How complicated is it to abuse it?

00:01:30.070 --> 00:01:33.910
And even for themselves, how hard is it to
fix it?

00:01:33.910 --> 00:01:38.770
And then they make an educated guess how
big the chance is that people are going to

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:44.230
abuse it. And that makes it a high-
priority or a low-priority fix.

00:01:44.230 --> 00:01:49.710
But nobody thought about AI that just does
a three-minute scan and then

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:52.110
says, hey, I know how to solve things

00:01:52.110 --> 00:01:58.070
for you. It goes further than that, because when
they publish a fix, the hackers

00:01:58.070 --> 00:02:03.690
are going to reverse engineer it, scanning
the Internet for which services

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:09.950
are running a version not updated yet, to
exploit what they fixed in the

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:11.310
most recent patch.

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:14.910
Yes. You are in a race with the hackers
all the time.

00:02:14.910 --> 00:02:15.710
It's crazy.

00:02:15.710 --> 00:02:19.810
And there are so many companies that still
have the old software running on the

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:23.710
server. I think that most people that come
into the company now don't even

00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:26.200
know what it is. They just know: don't
touch it.

00:02:26.200 --> 00:02:27.510
Don't take the power off.

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:32.930
It is still working. And if those servers
are connected to the Internet, they are

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:35.470
not getting security patches or anything
anymore.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:37.270
They are all end of life.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:41.990
And I still think that they are just
hoping nothing goes wrong.

00:02:41.990 --> 00:02:46.450
The bug sits in code that every Linux
server runs when it accepts the IPv4

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:50.750
TCP connection, before any application
sees the connection.

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.670
Anthropic reported that a Claude model
wrote a working proof-of-concept exploit

00:02:55.670 --> 00:02:59.710
that runs commands as root on a Linux
server with a multi-

00:02:59.710 --> 00:03:00.750
Jesus.

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:03.430
So this is big.

00:03:03.430 --> 00:03:05.970
It can take over the entire server.

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:12.110
And Linux is built into so many software
tools for running servers.

00:03:12.110 --> 00:03:14.440
Debian, Ubuntu, this one.

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.990
And everything is patched, but Ubuntu
didn't.

00:03:18.990 --> 00:03:23.070
And I was just searching for it, and I
think they did it six hours ago, but it

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:24.430
took them a few days.

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:30.060
So every server using Ubuntu, and most
people don't even know that the software

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:35.230
is running on Ubuntu, was open to the
entire Internet for takeover.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:36.150
Jesus.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.730
And then the other issue is

00:03:37.730 --> 00:03:39.430
that most people say, yeah,

00:03:39.430 --> 00:03:41.590
Am I hacked? OK, we checked everything.

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:47.820
There is no PHP file or any other file on
the server, but hackers don't just

00:03:47.820 --> 00:03:50.030
instantly put everything on it.

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:54.070
The only thing they do is create a
backdoor, store additional credentials,

00:03:54.070 --> 00:03:57.430
reuse your password, and try to break in.

00:03:57.430 --> 00:04:02.040
And once they know that they can go in,
they store it, and they take two

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:03.910
weeks, three weeks, four weeks.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:07.410
And that's when they go and test
everything, see how your backups are

00:04:07.410 --> 00:04:11.510
running. And once they have everything,
that's the moment when they take over.

00:04:11.510 --> 00:04:17.270
So they are sure that you are not able to
just restore a backup because then it's no

00:04:17.270 --> 00:04:22.080
longer hacked. Once they have the backups,
the security, everything in their own

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:26.470
pocket, that's the moment when they just
put on this black screen and say, we want

00:04:26.470 --> 00:04:29.870
to get free Bitcoins from you for giving
you back access.

00:04:29.870 --> 00:04:32.030
Because it locked the backup and stuff.

00:04:32.030 --> 00:04:33.550
It's crazy.

00:04:33.550 --> 00:04:37.630
This is also why you don't put a backup in
the building next door.

00:04:37.630 --> 00:04:43.140
For you, what was the process for all of
those PHP version

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:44.310
issues?

00:04:44.310 --> 00:04:46.310
We have different customers.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:51.010
So we have big customers who are on a
single VPS, and while the entire company

00:04:51.010 --> 00:04:53.950
runs on the VPS, I could not take it
offline.

00:04:53.950 --> 00:04:59.770
So what we did is we put a firewall in
front of it, and we only gave access to

00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:03.790
the server if you were logged in on a
remote desktop or on a VPN.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:08.490
So we are sure that only the people in the
office were able to access the server, and

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:13.820
that didn't fix the possible hack, but it
made sure that nobody could

00:05:13.820 --> 00:05:17.550
access the server, and we were safe until
we patched it.

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:22.510
And then we have hosting servers with 20
to 50 websites on them.

00:05:22.510 --> 00:05:24.400
What I did first was

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.070
I think around 1 p.m. I took them offline.

00:05:27.070 --> 00:05:29.830
So they have been offline for three hours
just to be sure.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:34.760
And then we deleted PHP versions 7.4 to
8.1, because we

00:05:34.760 --> 00:05:38.870
have been warning customers for the last
two years that they need to update, and we

00:05:38.870 --> 00:05:41.270
now just deleted those versions.

00:05:41.270 --> 00:05:43.760
We put the server back online after the
patching.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:46.360
We checked every website if it was still
working.

00:05:46.360 --> 00:05:51.100
If it gave issues, we used AI to check the
logs from the server where the issues

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:55.250
were, and we contacted the customers to
see if we could help them update the

00:05:55.250 --> 00:05:56.830
website if it was needed.

00:05:56.830 --> 00:05:59.910
And we did that for 400 websites within 24
hours.

00:05:59.910 --> 00:06:01.190
Wow.

00:06:01.190 --> 00:06:03.270
All thanks to Grok and Claude AI.

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:05.950
Jesus.

00:06:05.950 --> 00:06:12.380
I think it also makes those apps safer,
because you fix errors like those

00:06:12.380 --> 00:06:17.150
that were wide open for someone who knows
where they are.

00:06:17.150 --> 00:06:17.990
Right. Yes.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:22.910
So all of the websites, your 400 websites,
are safer than yesterday.

00:06:22.910 --> 00:06:25.670
Yeah. Until the next one, and then you
will have to do that again.

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:29.070
But we are upgrading the Internet right
now.

00:06:29.070 --> 00:06:30.990
We are making it more secure.

00:06:30.990 --> 00:06:34.550
And I think that's also the difference between what
the two of us do and what bigger

00:06:34.550 --> 00:06:38.730
companies do. Because you have the bigger
companies who have, like, 10k of

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:41.470
